  <content>
  <section><title>Write Constraint Resources</title>
<para>Review the following information from a previous lesson. </para>
<title>Writing Equations Using Symbols</title>
<para> When writing statements using algebraic symbols, the following steps may be helpful. </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Read the problem. 
  <emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Identify the variables and known values. If needed, sketch a picture of the scenario.
  <emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write a sentence using the relationship among the values. 
  <emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Translate the sentence into an equation.
</para>
<para><emphasis>Example</emphasis></para>
<para>You are buying chicken and steak for a barbeque. Chicken costs $1.29/lb and steak costs $3.49/lb.</para>

<!--Q1-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        What variables might you need to use in this scenario?
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here are some examples.</para>
    <list>
      <item>
        Let \(c\) represent the number of pounds of chicken that you can buy.
      </item>
      <item>
        Let \(s\) represent the number of pounds of steak that you can buy.
      </item>
      <item>
        Let \(T\) represent the total cost of the barbeque.
      </item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q2-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        What sentence might you use to describe the total cost of the barbeque?
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here are some examples.</para>
    <list>
      <item>
        The total cost of the barbeque is the sum of the total cost of all the chicken purchased and the total cost of all the steak purchased.
      </item>
      <item>
        The cost per pound of the chicken multiplied by the number of pounds purchased plus the cost per pound of the steak multiplied by the number of pounds purchased equals the total cost of the barbeque.
      </item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q3-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        Write an equation to show the total cost, \(T\), of the meat.
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(T = 1.29c + 3.49s\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q4-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        You have $100 to spend on the barbeque. Use this information and rewrite the equation \(T = 1.29c + 3.49s\) to find the number of pounds of chicken, \(c\), that you can buy.
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(c= \frac{100-3.49s}{1.29}\)</para>
    <para>
      \(T=1.29c+3.49s\)
      \(100=1.29c+3.49s\)
      \(100-3.49s=1.29c+3.49s-3.49s\)
      \(100-3.49s= 1.29c\)
      \(\frac{100-3.49s}{1.29}=\frac{ 1.29c}{1.29}\)
      \(\frac{100-3.49s}{1.29} =c\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q5-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        You have $100 to spend on the barbeque. Use this information and rewrite the equation \(T = 1.29c + 3.49s\) to find the number of pounds of steak, \(s\), that you can buy.
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(s=\frac{ 100-1.29c}{3.49}\)</para>
    <para>\(T=1.29c+3.49s\)
      \(100=1.29c+3.49s\)
      \(100-1.29c=1.29c-1.29c+3.49s\)
      \(100-1.29c=3.49s\)
      \(\frac{100-1.29c}{3.49}=\frac{3.49s}{3.49}\)
      \(s= \frac{100-1.29c}{3.49}\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<title>Try It: Write Constraint Equations</title>

<!--Q#-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>On a canoeing trip, you walk 4 mph then canoe at 7 mph and cover 30 miles. Write an equation to find how many miles you travel while canoeing.</para>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para><emphasis>STEP 1:</emphasis> Identify known variables.
      \(c\): miles canoeing
      \(w\): miles walked</para>
    <para><emphasis>STEP 2:</emphasis> Write an equation to show the distance covered.
      4w+7c=30</para>
    <para><emphasis>STEP 3:</emphasis> Write an equation that solves for \(c\) the number of miles traveled while canoeing.
      \(c=\frac{30-4}{w7}\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
